{"name":"Gooseberry","hex":"#a23f5d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gooseberry","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fruit of species Ribes uva-crispa, related to the currant.","Any other plant or fruit in the subgenus Grossularia, distinguished from currants by bearing spines, including Ribes hirtellum (American gooseberry)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gooseberry"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gooseberry","blocks":[{"text":"Gooseberry is a common name for many species of Ribes, as well as a large number of plants of similar appearance, and also several unrelated plants. The term gooseberry has a debated etymology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Stachelbeeren.jpg/330px-Stachelbeeren.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gooseberry","wikidata":"Q17638951"},{"title":"Gooseberry (gene)","blocks":[{"text":"Gooseberry (gsb) is a segment polarity gene located on chromosome 2 of the Drosophila genome. Gooseberry is known for its interactions with key embryonic signaling pathways Wingless and Hedgehog. The gene also has clinical significance, being linked to diseases such as Waardenburg Syndrome and rhabdomyosarcoma."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Gooseberry_Protein_3D_Structure.png/330px-Gooseberry_Protein_3D_Structure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gooseberry_(gene)","wikidata":"Q25203405"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Stachelbeere_%28Ribes_uva-crispa%29.jpg/500px-Stachelbeere_%28Ribes_uva-crispa%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Stachelbeere_%28Ribes_uva-crispa%29.jpg/500px-Stachelbeere_%28Ribes_uva-crispa%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.or